CT-5 negative-control contract for the negative_control_retroactive fixture
(window 2026-07-01..2026-07-31, as_of=2026-07-31, window_days=30):

  reopen_rate.py MUST exclude the retroactive-registration item (NB-001)
  from BOTH the numerator and denominator of its type (Bug), LIST it by id
  and reason, and still PASS (exit 0) with the correctly-adjusted rate:

    Bug:     { "reopened": 0, "closed": 2, "rate": 0.0,
               "excluded_retroactive": [
                 { "item_id": "NB-001",
                   "reason": "retroactive-registration (Opened->closure db_write gap < 60s)" }
               ] }
    Task:    { "reopened": 0, "closed": 1, "rate": 0.0 }
    Feature: { "reopened": 0, "closed": 1, "rate": 0.0 }
    overall: { "reopened": 0, "closed": 4, "rate": 0.0,
               "excluded_retroactive": [ { "item_id": "NB-001", ... } ] }

  Exit code: 0.

WHY THIS IS A NEGATIVE CONTROL, NOT A GOLDEN-BAD REFUSAL CASE: this fixture
is superficially similar in shape to golden_bad_mismatched_populations/
(both are "a window with 3 normal Bug items plus one item that needs
special handling"), but the two MUST produce OPPOSITE verdicts on their
own respective 'special' item:

  - golden_bad_mismatched_populations/MB-004 has NO closure event anywhere
    in its history -- reopen_rate.py cannot safely determine whether it
    was ever really "closed" in any sense this tool's data can see, so it
    MUST REFUSE (RATE_NOT_COMPUTABLE) and name the ambiguity.

  - THIS fixture's NB-001 DOES have a genuine closure event (Fixed,
    on_date=2026-07-05) -- it is simply a KNOWN, WELL-UNDERSTOOD data
    condition (an Opened->closure created_at gap of 30 seconds, reusing
    the EXACT [0,60)-second rule cycle_report.py's flag_retroactive_
    registration and select_sample.py's detect_retroactive_registration
    already implement and that this fixture's own item_history rows are
    deliberately modelled to trigger). There is nothing ambiguous about
    NB-001 -- it is a retroactive registration, a routine and expected
    occurrence (research/R1_baseline_cycle_time.md line 52: "Open->close
    'spans' of 0-10 s ... are retroactive registrations, not work
    durations"), and the correct response is to exclude-and-recompute,
    never to refuse the whole type's rate over it.

  A reopen_rate.py that conflated these two cases -- either refusing
  Bug's rate here too (treating a well-understood retroactive
  registration as if it were an unresolvable population mismatch), or
  silently including NB-001 in golden_bad_mismatched_populations' MB-004
  case (treating an unresolvable ambiguity as if it were a routine,
  safely-excludable retroactive registration) -- would fail this
  self-validation triple's whole point: the SAME general shape of input
  ("one item out of four needs special handling") must be correctly
  discriminated into two DIFFERENT required outcomes.

MUST NOT appear anywhere in the output for the Bug type or the overall
block: the naive-buggy value 0.3333333333333333 (or any rounding of it) in
place of the correct 0.0; "RATE_NOT_COMPUTABLE" anywhere (this fixture's
retroactive exclusion is a routine, safely-resolvable adjustment, not a
refusal case); NB-001 silently dropped with NO excluded_retroactive entry
naming it (the exclusion must be VISIBLE and AUDITABLE, exactly as
select_sample.py already lists its own bulk/retroactive exclusions on the
item rather than silently dropping the item from its output entirely).

Paired-mutation targets this fixture protects against (T093): counting
retroactive registrations as reopens (task line's own T093 wording) --
i.e. including NB-001's Reopened event in the numerator without excluding
NB-001 first, which would emit Bug rate=0.333... instead of the correct
0.0; silently excluding NB-001 with no excluded_retroactive entry (hides
the adjustment from an operator reading the report); refusing (RATE_NOT_
COMPUTABLE) instead of correctly recomputing (conflates this negative
control with the sibling golden-bad fixture's required verdict).
